      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2014 John Wiley & Sons A/S. Published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": "Carotid-cavernous fistula (CCF) is a pathologic communication between internal carotid artery (ICA) and cavernous sinus (CS). CCF occurs most commonly in association with craniofacial trauma. Traumatic CCFs are very rare, occurring in 0.17-0.27% of craniomaxillofacial trauma cases. We present a case of the patient treated for multiple facial fractures, who developed symptoms of CCF with several days latency and was successfully treated by endovascular occlusion of ICA. Anatomy of CS, pathophysiology of CCFs and treatment options are concisely reviewed."
      },
